+# Decision: Whole-visit revisions for concurrent visit edits
+
+## Context
+
+TidePlot's record store must define what happens when two field workers independently edit the same visit record. Workers share 12 tablets, can be offline for 48 hours, and sometimes correct the same visit after comparing field notes in the evening. Arrival order at the server is not evidence of authority — the worker who submitted later is not necessarily the one who saw the fresher field notes. Every correction is a scientific observation, and the HLD's tenets forbid silently discarding a submitted edit or making the path from submission to exported value uninspectable. Two coordinators resolve questions about submitted observations; the team has no dedicated systems administrator.
+
+The concurrency policy is not local to this node. It determines what tablet-sync must store and retry offline, what the review workflow shows coordinators, and which record states may enter the Friday CSV export. Choosing it here fixes those contracts' shapes.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+**Gates**
+
+- **No silent discard** (inherited gate; HLD tenet "Do not silently discard a submitted edit"): every submission must remain retrievable regardless of outcome. Any approach that overwrites or drops a payload is eliminated.
+
+**Weighted criteria**
+
+- **Whole-visit scientific integrity** (major; HLD tenet "Keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable"): an edit to a visit is an edit to a unit — a species identification and its cover estimate, or a date and an observer list, can be scientifically linked in ways field names do not declare. The policy must not let the server combine edits in ways a worker did not intend.
+- **Review workload sustainability** (major; HLD tenet "Prefer workflows two coordinators can operate without a dedicated systems administrator"): the volume of coordinator review must stay operable by two people at ~6,000 visits per season.
+- **Implementation and maintenance burden** (major): rules the server maintains must be understandable and changeable without a dedicated systems administrator.
+- **Export readiness** (moderate; goal "Make reviewable problems visible before the Friday export"): the share of visits that reach the partner's CSV without manual attention.
+- **Submission size and tablet storage** (minor): tablets are the constrained devices; the policy determines what they must retain and transmit.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Whole-visit revisions
+
+**Description**: Each submission is a complete visit payload naming the base revision it was written against. The server stores every accepted submission as an immutable revision. A submission based on the current tip applies automatically. A submission based on a stale base becomes a *competing revision*: the visit is marked unresolved, and a coordinator later selects one revision as the winning current state. The server never compares payloads or combines anything; the only conflict test is the base-mismatch test, which is deterministic and cheap.
+
+Tablet-sync retains the base revision ID with each local edit, displays pending competing revisions to the worker, and sizes its retry state machine around the revision envelope. Review-export needs a revision-comparison screen for competing sets and holds unresolved visits out of the CSV export.
+
+**Demonstration**: Worker A bases on revision 3 and corrects an observer name; worker B also bases on revision 3 and adds a missing species. Whichever arrives second is stored as revision 5 and flagged competing against revision 4; a coordinator compares the two complete visits and selects one.
+
+**Analysis**:
+- No silent discard: **passes** — all revisions are retained immutably.
+- Whole-visit scientific integrity: **strong** — the server never composes payloads; every submitted visit reaches a coordinator intact or becomes current intact.
+- Review workload: **partial** — even unrelated-field edits (an observer-name fix plus a species addition) create competing review work, so the queue grows with correction traffic.
+- Implementation burden: **strong** — one deterministic test and one resolution operation; no per-field rules to maintain.
+- Export readiness: **partial** — unresolved visits are excluded from export until a coordinator resolves them.
+- Tablet cost: **strong** — tablets keep a base revision ID per edit, not a field-delta machinery.
+
+**Summary**: Trades a larger coordinator review queue for zero server-side merge logic and intact scientific units.
+
+### Field-aware three-way merge
+
+**Description**: Each submission supplies its base state and structured field-level changes. The server applies per-field merge rules: changes to disjoint fields combine automatically into a new revision; overlapping changes (the same field touched from the same base) are flagged for review. Review presents field-level provenance — which worker changed which field — rather than whole revisions.
+
+Tablet-sync retains base snapshots and transmits structured changes rather than complete visits. Review-export needs a field-provenance review view and reports per-field outcomes.
+
+**Demonstration**: Worker A corrects an observer name; worker B adds a species, both from the same base. The rules see disjoint fields and combine them automatically into a current record; no review occurs.
+
+**Analysis**:
+- No silent discard: **passes** — originals are retained; combined records are derived views.
+- Whole-visit scientific integrity: **partial** — automatic combination is exactly the risk: rules for scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries cannot know that a revised species identification is scientifically related to its cover estimate, and may combine edits whose relationship the rules do not understand.
+- Review workload: **strong** — only overlapping edits reach coordinators; queue volume drops well below whole-visit's.
+- Implementation burden: **partial** — a maintained rule set per field type (scalars, lists, species entries) that grows with every new field and must stay correct; the team has no dedicated systems administrator.
+- Export readiness: **strong** — far more visits become export-ready without manual attention.
+- Tablet cost: **partial** — tablets retain base snapshots and implement structured change extraction.
+
+**Summary**: Trades maintained merge rules and a subtle scientific-integrity risk for a smaller review queue and better export throughput.
+
+## Selection
+
+Whole-visit revisions wins. The decision turns on the tenet **"Keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable"**, weighted through the whole-visit-scientific-integrity criterion: the team's stated priority is that the server must never combine scientifically related edits without noticing, and the coordinators would rather work a larger review queue than maintain merge rules whose blind spots are invisible to them. Implementation burden (also major) points the same way — whole-visit needs one deterministic test where three-way merge needs a maintained rules library.
+
+The costs accepted: review volume rises with correction traffic, and unresolved visits delay the Friday export for those visits. Both costs are visible on the surface (a coordinator sees the competing set; an unresolved visit is explicitly flagged), which keeps them operable rather than silent.
+
+**Why the other approach remains live**: field-aware three-way merge is not settled-and-dead. If the coordinator review queue proves unsustainable for two people — measured, not assumed — or if unresolved visits routinely block the Friday export, this decision should be revisited. What would flip it: evidence that most competing sets involve genuinely unrelated field edits, and a merge-rule design that surfaces (rather than silently performs) automatic combinations for coordinator confirmation. What would not flip it: transient frustration with review volume alone. Any reversal must keep the no-silent-discard gate and would need to re-derive tablet-sync's and review-export's contracts.
+
+**Downstream accommodations committed by this decision**:
+
+- *Tablet-sync*: retains the base revision ID with every local edit; sends complete visit payloads (not deltas); displays pending competing revisions to the worker; sizes local storage and its retry state machine around the revision envelope.
+- *Review-export*: builds a revision-comparison view over competing sets; records resolution outcomes (winner, coordinator, contributing revisions); holds unresolved visits out of the CSV export set; sources provenance for exported values from the revision history.
